# Copy to .env (never commit .env) before `docker compose up`.
#
# The database password is not here: scripts/init_secrets.sh writes it to smo/secrets/db_password, which compose
# mounts as a secret (PR-SEC-4.3).

# Optional. Postgres logs every statement slower than this many milliseconds (-1 turns it off, 0 logs all).
# POSTGRES_SLOW_QUERY_MS=500

# Optional (SEC-15.18): encrypt the SME identities (module and rApp-instance invoker secrets) kept in the database. SMO_IDENTITY_KEY (at least 32 characters,
# `openssl rand -base64 32`) is passed to every service, so all replicas share it; set it, restart, then run once
# `docker compose exec rapp-mgmt python -m smo_shared.module_identity encrypt-existing` for the rows written before. Back the key up apart from the database (a restore without it
# cannot read them). To rotate: new key in SMO_IDENTITY_KEY, the old one in SMO_IDENTITY_PREVIOUS_KEY, restart, run the command again, then remove the previous key. docs/SECRETS.md.
# SMO_IDENTITY_KEY=
# SMO_IDENTITY_PREVIOUS_KEY=

# Optional (PR-HA-6.2, docs/DISASTER_RECOVERY.md). Off-site backups to an S3-compatible bucket: `docker compose --profile backup up -d --build db-backup`.
# The service refuses to run without the bucket. Leave the endpoint empty for AWS S3 (and the keys empty with an instance role); for MinIO give its URL.
# SMO_BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=smo-backups
# SMO_BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=smo
# SMO_BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
# SMO_BACKUP_S3_SSE=AES256
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-east-1
# SMO_BACKUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=600     # the RPO is this plus one backup's duration: keep it well under 900
# SMO_BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14
# SMO_BACKUP_KEEP_MIN=5

# Optional. The address consumers outside the compose network reach R1 Termination by. Unset, /bootstrap names SME on the
# compose network (http://sme:8000). With the tls profile set it to the HTTPS door, e.g. https://localhost:8443, and /bootstrap
# advertises https://localhost:8443/sme/... instead (PR-SEC-1.6). An origin only: no path.
# R1_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://localhost:8443

# Optional (PR-SEC-9.3). Set, R1 Termination's GET /bootstrap needs the header X-Bootstrap-Key with this value (401 without), and every
# module and rApp that compose starts sends it. Unset, /bootstrap is open, as before. A gate against scanners, not an identity: see
# SECURITY.md. Past a trial use R1_BOOTSTRAP_KEY_FILE (and SMO_BOOTSTRAP_KEY_FILE in the clients) with a secret, not an environment variable.
# R1_BOOTSTRAP_KEY=

# Optional (PR-SEC-8.5). `memory` (default): each R1 Termination replica rate-limits for itself. `postgres`: the per-caller buckets live in
# the database, so all replicas share one budget (one UPSERT per authenticated request; fails open to a per-replica bucket if the database errs).
# R1_RATE_STORE=memory

# Optional (PR-SEC-6): OIDC login for the operator GUI, next to the local username/password login (kept as break-glass admin).
# The redirect URI is the public URL of the GUI's /api/oidc/callback, registered at the provider; the group map turns the provider's groups
# (the claim GUI_OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM of the ID token) into the roles viewer/operator/admin; a user in no mapped group is refused unless
# GUI_OIDC_DEFAULT_ROLE is set. Multi-factor sign-in is the provider's to enforce. gui-bff/README.md, "OIDC login".
# GUI_OIDC_ENABLED=true
# GUI_OIDC_ISSUER=https://keycloak.example.com/realms/smo
# GUI_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=smo-gui
# GUI_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GUI_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=https://gui.example.com/api/oidc/callback
# GUI_OIDC_GROUP_ROLE_MAP=smo-admins=admin,smo-ops=operator,smo-viewers=viewer
# GUI_OIDC_PROVIDER_NAME=Keycloak
# GUI_LOCAL_LOGIN_ENABLED=false     # only once the provider is the one way in by policy

# Optional (SEC-15.19, docs/SECRETS.md). Rotate the secret of running rApp instances on a schedule: the rapp-mgmt-worker renews the secret of every running instance
# (with an operator API) that is older than this many days. Unset or 0 rotates nothing, which is what an upgrade does.
# RAPP_MGMT_SECRET_ROTATION_DAYS=90
# RAPP_MGMT_SECRET_ROTATION_MAX_PER_RUN=10      # at most this many per run, oldest first
# RAPP_MGMT_SECRET_ROTATION_SWEEP_SECONDS=900   # how often the worker looks

# Optional (PR-DB-3, docs/RETENTION.md). How many days a high-volume table keeps its rows. Unset or 0 keeps everything, which is what an upgrade
# does: nothing is deleted until you uncomment a number. The values below are the periods PROPOSED in docs/RETENTION.md for production. They are
# proposals, not legal advice (docs/PRIVACY.md): confirm them, or write your own, before you uncomment them. The purges run in the ran-nf-oam-worker,
# mdaf-worker and dme-worker services (hourly); the GUI audit log is purged by a command (see GUI_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS).
# SMO_RETENTION_ALARMS_DAYS=90            # alarms cleared more than this many days ago (a raised alarm is never removed)
# SMO_RETENTION_PM_FILES_DAYS=14          # PM files (the measurements are the row); keep at least the longest KPI look-back
# SAFEGUARD_REFUSAL_RETENTION_DAYS=90     # recorded safeguard refusals
# Not proposed (docs/RETENTION.md says keep), listed so the choice is visible. Both are written to the audit chain, which these never touch.
# SMO_RETENTION_APPROVALS_DAYS=0          # rApp action approvals that were decided or lapsed more than this many days ago (a request still waiting is never removed)
# SMO_RETENTION_DECISION_RECORDS_DAYS=0   # decision records (why an rApp acted) older than this and already hashed into the audit chain
# SMO_RETENTION_MDAF_REPORTS_DAYS=30      # MDAF analytics reports
# SMO_RETENTION_DME_RECORDS_DAYS=30       # DME data records (keep at least the longest training window a consumer reads)
# GUI_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=365            # the GUI audit log: `docker compose exec gui-bff python -m app.retention`, from cron on the host
# GUI_AUDIT_EXPORT_DIR=/data/audit-export # with this set the rows are written there (inside the gui_bff_data volume) as JSON lines before they are deleted
# (The outbox and idempotency-key rows of that table already expire after one day by default; compose does not pass those two settings through.)
# A table whose retention is left at 0 is reported once it is large: the worker logs a WARNING a day and exports smo_retention_off_rows{table} above this
# many rows (0 = never warn).
# SMO_RETENTION_WARN_ROWS=1000000
# Optional (PR-SEC-7): multi-factor sign-in. GUI_TOTP_KEY (at least 32 characters, `openssl rand -base64 32`) lets local accounts enrol a one-time code
# (Account security in the GUI) and encrypts the stored secrets; losing or changing it means resetting every user's code. GUI_LOGIN_MODE=oidc closes the
# password form to every account but a break-glass one (needs the OIDC settings above). GUI_ADMIN_MFA_REQUIRED=true makes a local admin enrol a code first.
# gui-bff/README.md, "Multi-factor sign-in".
# GUI_TOTP_KEY=
# GUI_ADMIN_MFA_REQUIRED=true
# GUI_LOGIN_MODE=oidc
# Optional (PR-SEC-5): sign the GUI session token with a key pair instead of GUI_JWT_SECRET. GUI_JWT_ALGORITHM=RS256 or ES256 and GUI_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE (a PEM file mounted
# into the gui-bff container by an override); GUI_JWT_PREVIOUS_KEY_FILES (comma separated) keeps the keys of earlier rotations verifying. Changing the algorithm signs everyone out.
# gui-bff/README.md, "Signing keys". The gateway's cache of token checks: R1_INTROSPECTION_CACHE_SECONDS (30 by default; 0 = off), r1-termination/README.md.
# GUI_JWT_ALGORITHM=ES256
# GUI_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/gui_jwt_private_key
# GUI_JWT_PREVIOUS_KEY_FILES=
# R1_INTROSPECTION_CACHE_SECONDS=30
# Optional (PR-RAPP-1): signed rApp packages. ONBOARDING_TRUST_STORE is a path INSIDE the onboarding container to a PEM public-key file or a directory of <publisher>.pub files;
# mount your directory there with a docker-compose.override.yml (docs/RAPP_PACKAGING.md section 8). Empty (the default): no signature is checked. ONBOARDING_REQUIRE_SIGNED_PACKAGES=true
# refuses an unsigned package. The sample packages are signed with a DEMO key whose private half is public (samples/demo-signing): never trust it in production.
# ONBOARDING_TRUST_STORE=/run/rapp-trust
# ONBOARDING_REQUIRE_SIGNED_PACKAGES=false
